# Interpret → Plan → Permission (Mandatory)

Before **editing files**, **running destructive or broad commands**, or **starting multi-step implementation**, you MUST post a short gate message and **wait for explicit user approval**. Do not skip this because the task seems obvious or urgent.

## Required gate format

Use exactly these three sections (in order):

### 1. My interpretation
- What you believe the user is asking for
- What problem they care about (not just the literal words)
- What is **in scope** vs **out of scope** for this turn

### 2. Planned approach
- **How this protects SSOT:** Plain-language explanation of how the plan respects single source of truth — one canonical path, no parallel preview/commit/hover/sim owner; what obsolete path gets deleted in the same change. **Not** a file name alone.
- **Heuristics added:** `none` or list each (**only** after walking the 6-row audit in `global-systems-first.mdc`)
- **Why (not) heuristic:** one brief sentence — why `none` is justified, or why each named item is heuristic
- Concrete steps you will take (files, systems, tests)
- What you will **not** do (important when past mistakes are relevant)
- **Any edit type** (bug fix, feature, refactor, perf, rules): defer/throttle/pending staging is forbidden unless ⚠ exception approved — not only lag tasks
- Risks or assumptions, if any
- If this plan **breaks or bypasses** a global system → include the **⚠ Global system exception** block from `global-systems-first.mdc` and wait for approval on the exception (not just the plan)
- **Will not add this turn:** parallel preview/commit/sim path; UI-only flag/meta sim ignores; `if ability.id` / `if node ==` / scene/tab branch when data or shared pipeline could express it; duplicate logic without deleting the obsolete path in the **same** change — unless ⚠ exception block approved.
- **Lag / feel / parity** (when slowness or “feels different” — e.g. premove vs postmove): before any perf tweak, state fast vs slow scenario and whether they use the **same** owner API; unify first. Perf-specific bans: `.cursor/rules/minimal-performance-impact.mdc` § Forbidden on planning / combat.

### 3. Permission
End with a direct question, e.g. **"Proceed with this plan? (yes / no / adjust)"**

Wait for the user's reply before making changes or running implementation work.

## After implementation (mandatory)

Responses that **completed** file changes must **repeat** all three lines (not only in the gate):

- **How this protects SSOT:** …
- **Heuristics added:** `none` or list each
- **Why (not) heuristic:** …

Place them in **## Changelog → ### Single source of truth** (see `global-systems-first.mdc`). **`none` without a substantive Why line is invalid.** **SSOT line that only names a file is invalid.**

## When the gate is required

- Bug fixes, features, refactors, config/rule changes
- Git **pushes**, PRs (unless the user already said "push" / "open a PR" in the same message)
- Any response where your next step would modify the repo or run non-trivial commands

**Git commits are NOT gated** — mandatory full backups per `.cursor/rules/phase-commit.mdc` (absolute; no overrides).

## When the gate is optional

- Pure Q&A, explanations, or read-only investigation **when the user only asked a question** and did not ask you to implement
- User message already contains explicit approval: "go ahead", "do it", "yes proceed", "implement that", etc.
- User is answering your permission question from the previous turn (treat as approval to execute the stated plan)

## Anti-patterns (do not do these)

- Jumping straight to code because the fix "looks simple"
- **Ending an edit or fix turn without `Heuristics added:` and `Why (not) heuristic:`** (brief reason required; `none` is not a checkbox)
- **Writing `Heuristics added: none` without a substantive Why** (e.g. “no heuristics”, “audit passed”)
- Proposing a heuristic or bandaid without the **⚠ Global system exception** block when it bypasses a global system
- Solving a different problem than the user described (e.g. placement vs draw order)
- Assuming post-process vs pre-process without stating it and getting buy-in
- Bundling surprise scope (extra refactors, commits, new files) without listing them in the plan
- Any add/edit/refactor plan that skips **Will not add** parallel paths or ships `Heuristics added: none` without walking the 6-row audit
- Refactor/cleanup that adds a helper or file but leaves the old path live “for safety” without owner-approved deferral
- **How this protects SSOT** that only names a file/API with no explanation of what stays one path or what parallel path was avoided
